---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, AUTHOR_NAME } from '../consts';

export const prerender = true;

const layers = [
	{
		n: 1,
		name: 'Retrieval',
		question: 'Can the engine reach and read your pages at all?',
		fails: [
			'The page renders client-side and the crawler receives an empty shell. Most AI crawlers do not execute JavaScript.',
			'robots.txt allows the bot, but a WAF or bot-fight rule returns 403 to the same user agent.',
			'The server returns less content to a bot than to a browser — not a block, a quiet downgrade.',
			'The page exists but is in no sitemap and nothing links to it.',
		],
		measure:
			'Request the page as each bot and compare the response, word count and status code against what a browser receives. A 200 is not enough; the body has to match.',
		fix: 'Server-side render or pre-render the pages that matter. Allow search and user-triggered bots at the firewall, not just in robots.txt. Verify bots by IP range instead of blocking the user agent.',
	},
	{
		n: 2,
		name: 'Citation',
		question: 'Does the engine use your content as a source?',
		fails: [
			'The page is retrievable but answers nothing a buyer asks, so nothing pulls it into an answer.',
			'A competitor covers the same question with more sources pointing at it.',
			'The content is correct but undated, so the engine prefers a fresher-looking source.',
			'The claim worth citing is buried in the middle of a long page instead of stated plainly.',
		],
		measure:
			'Run the buyer question and record which URLs the engine cites. Yours either appears or it does not. Per engine, because they disagree.',
		fix: 'Write the page that answers the question directly, state the citable claim early, give it a visible date, and earn mentions on pages you do not control.',
	},
	{
		n: 3,
		name: 'Interpretation',
		question: 'Does the engine understand what your content says?',
		fails: [
			'The page says what you do but never names the category, so the model infers one.',
			'Your title, meta description, H1, schema and llms.txt each describe the company differently. The model picks one.',
			'Two live domains split the citations, and neither accumulates enough to settle the record.',
			'Wikidata, a directory profile, or an old press release carries a category you abandoned — and it has more sources behind it than your homepage.',
		],
		measure:
			'Compare every field on your own site against each other first. Then compare the consensus of your site against what third-party sources say. Disagreement is the finding.',
		fix: 'One description of the company, repeated identically everywhere you control. Then correct the third-party records, starting with the ones that have the most citations pointing at them.',
	},
	{
		n: 4,
		name: 'Expression',
		question: 'How does the engine describe you in the final answer?',
		fails: [
			'You are cited accurately and described in the category you left two years ago.',
			'You are mentioned in a list of ten, which is not the same as being recommended.',
			'The description is accurate but aimed at a buyer you do not sell to.',
			'The engine names a competitor as the answer and uses your page as the source for why.',
		],
		measure:
			'Ask the question your buyer would ask, with their constraints attached, five times per engine. Record who was recommended, in what order, and how you were described when you appeared.',
		fix: 'This layer is downstream. It moves when the first three are resolved and the pages that decide the answer exist — comparisons, alternatives, integrations, "best for" pages built around constrained questions.',
	},
];

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'Article',
	headline: 'The four layers of AI answer failure',
	description:
		'A framework for diagnosing why a company is missing or misdescribed in AI-generated answers: retrieval, citation, interpretation, and expression.',
	author: { '@id': 'https://www.aeogrowth.co/#gerald' },
	publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.aeogrowth.co/#business' },
	mainEntityOfPage: 'https://www.aeogrowth.co/framework',
	about: layers.map((l) => ({
		'@type': 'DefinedTerm',
		name: l.name,
		description: l.question,
	})),
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`The four layers of AI answer failure — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Retrieval, citation, interpretation, expression. A framework for diagnosing why a company is missing or misdescribed in AI-generated answers, and what each layer takes to fix."
		/>
		<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} is:inline />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<main>
			<article class="prose">
				<p class="eyebrow">Framework</p>
				<h1>The four layers of AI answer failure</h1>

				<p class="lead">
					"We don't show up in ChatGPT" is four different problems wearing the same sentence. They
					have different causes, different tests, and different fixes. Working on the wrong one is
					the most common way companies waste a year on this.
				</p>

				<p>
					An engine does four things between a buyer's question and the answer on screen. It reaches
					your pages. It decides whether to use them as a source. It forms an understanding of what
					your company is. And it writes a description of you into the answer. Each step can fail
					independently, and each one can fail while the step before it worked perfectly.
				</p>

				<p>
					Most tools in this category measure the first two and report a percentage. The failures
					that cost pipeline tend to live in the last two — where a model reads your page, takes a
					fact from it, recommends a competitor, and describes you as the company you stopped being.
				</p>

				<h2>Why the order matters</h2>

				<p>
					The layers are sequential. Content published on top of an unresolved identity does not
					compound — it gets filed under whichever record the model already settled on, and adds
					weight to the wrong one. If the model thinks you are a chatbot company, ten excellent
					pages about AI agents become ten excellent pages by a chatbot company.
				</p>

				<p>
					So the diagnosis runs downward and the work runs upward. You find the lowest layer that is
					broken, and you fix from there.
				</p>

				{layers.map((l) => (
					<section class="layer">
						<h2>
							<span class="layer-n">{l.n}</span> {l.name}
						</h2>
						<p class="layer-q">{l.question}</p>

						<h3>How it fails</h3>
						<ul>
							{l.fails.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
						</ul>

						<h3>How to measure it</h3>
						<p>{l.measure}</p>

						<h3>What resolves it</h3>
						<p>{l.fix}</p>
					</section>
				))}

				<h2>Using it</h2>

				<p>
					Start at layer one, because it is the cheapest to test and the most common silent failure.
					Request your own page as a bot and compare it to what a browser gets. If the bot receives
					an empty shell or a 403, stop — nothing above that layer can work, and no amount of
					content will change it.
				</p>

				<p>
					If retrieval is clean, check whether your own site agrees with itself before looking
					anywhere else. Title, description, H1, schema, llms.txt. Internal disagreement is free to
					fix and it is where a surprising number of these cases end.
				</p>

				<p>
					Only then is it worth running buyer questions across engines. By that point, a bad answer
					tells you something real: the record describing you is wrong, or it is right and the pages
					that decide the answer do not exist yet.
				</p>

				<h2>What this framework does not cover</h2>

				<p>
					It describes failure modes, not timelines. Changes to your own pages register soonest in
					engines that search the web on every question. Changes to how third parties describe you
					depend on those sites updating and being re-crawled, which is outside anyone's control.
					Any framework that promises a date for a specific engine is guessing.
				</p>

				<p>
					It also does not cover whether the buyer wanted your product in the first place. Being
					described correctly is table stakes, not a strategy.
				</p>

				<hr />

				<p class="byline">
					Written by {AUTHOR_NAME}. You can test layers one and three on your own domain with
					<a href="/ai-crawler-check/">Crawler Check</a> and
					<a href="/positioning-check/">Positioning Check</a>, both free. The
					<a href="/services">audit</a> covers all four.
				</p>
			</article>
		</main>

		<Footer />

		<style>
			.layer { margin-top: 3.5rem; }
			.layer h2 { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.7rem; }
			.layer-n {
				display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
				width: 1.9rem; height: 1.9rem; flex: none;
				border-radius: 50%; border: 1px solid currentColor;
				font-size: 0.95rem; opacity: 0.55;
			}
			.layer-q { font-size: 1.1rem; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
			.layer h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.8rem; opacity: 0.6; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
			.byline { font-size: 0.95rem; opacity: 0.7; }
		</style>
	</body>
</html>
